/T (One server may store one copy, reducing capacity compared with three replicas. As users grow, its network, disk and metadata throughput become bottlenecks. Caching helps reads but writes converge on one server. A server failure can stop access until recovery unless failover and replicated storage exist. Distributed systems cost more but scale and tolerate node failure.)
/E (One server may store one copy, reducing capacity compared with three replicas. As users grow, its network, disk and metadata throughput become bottlenecks. Caching helps reads but writes converge on one server. A server failure can stop access until recovery unless failover and replicated storage exist. Distributed systems cost more but scale and tolerate node failure.)
